{
  "reviews": [
    {
      "id": "R1",
      "criteria": {
        "baseline_relative_value": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The installed expression \"draft_cost_ms[len(req_ids)]\" and reported budget-dependent draft descriptors establish a concrete cost-model question. Comparing against AV-off, unchanged AV and a locked static policy addresses the actual baseline. Net savings, consequential decision frequency and primary ITL effect remain unknown; the proposal appropriately makes them qualification questions."
        },
        "bounded_pilot_decision": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The eight-hour offline stage can resolve dispatch reconstruction and measurement-design feasibility without repeating the failed timing run. Conditional GPU stages retain all-rank adequacy, correctness, progress accounting and explicit inconclusive outcomes. Before release, the replay-state reset protocol, confidence provenance and numerical definition of the 2% opportunity proxy still need specification."
        },
        "engineering_time_and_fit": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Inspection, implementation/correctness and instrumentation have separate 8/32/16-hour caps, with an implementation checkpoint. Installed source identities and SM90 attention constraints are recognized. Whether joint-rank state is available without additional synchronization remains unknown; the capped feasibility stage appropriately precedes implementation. Provisional effort ranges are planning assumptions, not demonstrated estimates."
        },
        "evaluation_gpu_budget": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 5,
          "rationale": "The first stage requires zero GPU-hours. Three conditional 60-minute eight-GPU allocations correctly total 24 GPU-hours, include startup and evidence collection, and prohibit retries. Later screen/confirmation/held-out caps are separately 20/100/20 GPU-hours. Historical startup is acknowledged, and monetary cost is correctly parameterized as P times GPU-hours/8."
        },
        "evidence_and_history": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Reported observation: C24 remains \"instrumentation_inconclusive\". The CPU receipt explicitly establishes \"no measured milliseconds, dispatch or service gain.\" Supplied DeepInfer code demonstrates runtime cost correction, not this proposed distributed correction. The transfer inference is appropriately conditional, and neither C03 nor grouped C01 is reopened. The already completed synthetic check should be recorded as completed history."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "The CPU budgets 11 versus 3 use invented costs. They establish sensitivity under an assumed dependency, not a measured controller defect or material opportunity.",
        "The installed confidence-head path is conditional on adaptive verification. The supplied AV-off diagnostic does not establish availability of native confidence arrays for replay; synthetic fixtures and runtime confidence evidence must remain distinct.",
        "Matching joint-state sequences while policies produce different progress requires explicit state restoration and token accounting. Such component fixtures do not reproduce policy-dependent service trajectories.",
        "The GPU ceiling does not establish that calibration, four comparators, correctness and adequate captures fit within it. Exhaustion must remain inconclusive, without an automatic replacement allocation."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Retain R1 as the leading offline continuation of C24. Record the synthetic counterexample as already completed, then narrow the remaining work to joint-rank dispatch, deployable state access and the instrumentation addendum. Specify confidence provenance, replay resets, comparator scheduling and the uncertainty calculation for net savings per committed token. Preserve the existing GPU hold and original C24/C03 verdicts."
    },
    {
      "id": "R2",
      "criteria": {
        "baseline_relative_value": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "Installed code contains \"for i in range(n_spec):\", and the proposed fusion removes intermediate work while retaining bias GEMMs and sequential dependence. The four-hour membership and constructor-gate audit is an appropriate cheap eligibility test. Native incidence, watermark eligibility and savings inside existing graphs remain unknown; bundled GB300 percentages are correctly excluded from the H200 prediction."
        },
        "bounded_pilot_decision": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The staged source audit, standalone component qualification and all-rank integration can distinguish incompatibility, immaterial savings and inadequate evidence. Real-weight parity, RNG/cache checks, repeated graph replay and preserved watermark semantics are relevant. The follow-up now permits more specific synchronization review, but SM90 correctness and complete adapter behavior remain unqualified."
        },
        "engineering_time_and_fit": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "Separate 4/32/10-hour caps and an eight-hour feasibility checkpoint bound the uncertain port. TP1 supports investigating an unsharded scope, and the proposal excludes the specialized executor and vocabulary sharding. A semantics-preserving embedding accessor, actual watermark configuration and installed helper compatibility remain unresolved; these must be settled before substantial implementation."
        },
        "evaluation_gpu_budget": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 5,
          "rationale": "The initial audit uses zero GPU-hours. The 45+60+60-minute eight-GPU allocations correctly total 22 GPU-hours, including compilation, correctness, startup and collection. Later service budgets are separate, retries are excluded, and cap exhaustion defers advancement. This is a bounded ceiling, not evidence that qualification will finish within it."
        },
        "evidence_and_history": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The original missing-code assessment needs a dated update: the follow-up reports a complete retained extract, and newly supplied passages show \"sem=\\\"acq_rel\\\", scope=\\\"gpu\\\"\", counter reset and launcher dtype checks. However, the displayed source still contains excerpt gaps, so complete independent code review cannot be claimed. Watermarker construction remains unresolved despite the additional factory evidence."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "unknown",
      "objections": [
        "The blanket statement that complete reduction and launcher code is missing no longer accurately describes artifact availability. Distinguish the original packet limitation, the subsequently retained complete file and the portions actually visible in this supplied packet.",
        "The factory constructs a draft watermarker for supported algorithms. Request-level watermark=true proves neither that the constructor predicate passes nor that a target-only fallback is active; the exact algorithm and configuration remain unknown.",
        "Acquire/release operations and counter reset are visible implementation facts, not proof of safe cross-block publication, scratch reuse or graph replay on SM90.",
        "The upstream integer-weight tests do not establish real-checkpoint parity. Preserving the existing bias GEMM helps isolate the change but does not qualify cache rounding, vocabulary mapping or RNG behavior."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Append a follow-up assessment without rewriting the original card. Replace the obsolete availability blocker with a precise inventory of supplied synchronization/launcher evidence and remaining excerpt or integration gaps. Keep implementation held pending the effective draft-watermarker predicate and a specialization-independent adapter. Retain the four-hour zero-GPU audit; neither the retained file nor upstream results release GPU qualification."
    },
    {
      "id": "R3",
      "criteria": {
        "baseline_relative_value": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "The proposed causal path applies only to an active FP32-weight router branch. New checkpoint evidence reports three sampled weights as BF16, while nominal GateLinear requires \"self.weight.dtype == torch.float32\" for BF16x3 eligibility. This materially weakens applicability without proving loaded dtype. The remaining three-hour loader/cast audit is justified; optimization value remains unresolved."
        },
        "bounded_pilot_decision": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The pilot correctly stops for demonstrated ineligibility, equivalent optimization or scope-expanding precision changes. Numerical, routing-selection and graph checks precede timing, and missing coverage remains inconclusive. The first stage should now investigate installed loading and dispatch using the completed header evidence, rather than repeat the original broad eligibility search."
        },
        "engineering_time_and_fit": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The 3/20/8-hour allocations and included four-hour implementation checkpoint bound feasibility work. Hardware matches the upstream proposal, but the exact proposed kernel, installed loader behavior and local shape support remain unknown. The nominal BF16-weight/FP32-output path is a different existing implementation; targeting it would require a separately justified mechanism."
        },
        "evaluation_gpu_budget": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 5,
          "rationale": "The eligibility stage uses zero GPU-hours. The 30+60+60-minute eight-GPU allocations correctly total 20 GPU-hours, with startup, compilation, correctness and collection included. Separate later service budgets and no-retry limits are explicit. Given the new counterevidence, these GPU allocations remain conditional ceilings, not a recommended expenditure."
        },
        "evidence_and_history": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "The card correctly distinguishes FP32 output from FP32 weights, but its current assessment omits the new BF16-header and GateLinear evidence. The header artifact cautions: \"Stored checkpoint dtype does not establish final loaded parameter dtype.\" Upstream H200 results concern other models and remain author-reported. The supplied memory records offline applicability as inconclusive, not validated eligibility."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "The three sampled BF16 tensors are concrete counterevidence that must affect prioritization. Neither their dtype nor the existence of 43 matching keys establishes every router's loaded dtype.",
        "An FP32 output request does not select the FP32-weight branch. Installed allocation, loading, casts and forward dispatch must establish the proposed baseline delta.",
        "The sampled shapes are target 384×5120 and draft 128×5120. The supplied nominal FP32 GEMV shape table does not include those shapes; the upstream 24-token cutoff cannot be applied generically.",
        "The exact proposed kernel implementation remains absent from the supplied material. PR descriptions and other-model measurements do not establish local compilation, numerical behavior or service materiality."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Append the BF16-header findings and nominal GateLinear predicates, preserving the original assessment's date and scope. Downgrade this to a residual installed-loader/dispatch check with a three-hour CPU/source cap. Require evidence of existing FP32 loaded weights and an active eligible branch before porting; stop if BF16 loading is established. Do not cast weights merely to create eligibility or reserve GPU qualification yet."
    }
  ],
  "global_gaps": [
    "Priority is R1 offline measurement design, then R2 configuration/adapter inspection, then R3 residual loader inspection. None is ready for a full service attempt. Three reviewed proposals satisfy the requested upper bound; no fourth idea is needed.",
    "Candidate-specific exposed savings and frozen-primary ITL effects remain unknown. Row counts, invocation frequency and upstream percentages cannot independently establish the 2% opportunity proxy; its denominator, progress adjustment and uncertainty treatment need prospective definitions.",
    "Full G01-G05 and relevant quality-gate definitions are not supplied. Their missing thresholds must be resolved before GPU release, while preserving native workloads, seq64, the positive service screen, 2% promotion requirement, 3% tail guards and five paired confirmation blocks for small gains.",
    "C03 retains its unfavorable single screen without a measured causal explanation. C01 grouped performance remains an external unfavorable report without raw evidence. C24 remains completed and instrumentation-inconclusive; continuation must reuse its existing work item rather than duplicate a runtime campaign.",
    "Supplied DeepInfer pinned controller code is useful mechanism evidence. PR92 reports an extensive H20-3e Hopper port, not this H200 baseline; other supplied PR claims do not establish local membership or savings. Separate issue bodies and omitted implementation passages remain unavailable for this review.",
    "Engineering completion times, startup/calibration variability, node price and cost-per-token savings remain unknown. All GPU figures are future allocation caps; this review performs no tools, file changes, agent work or GPU execution."
  ]
}
